From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 12:47:30 +0200
Subject: media: cedrus: Add P010 output for 10-bit VP9 and H.265

For 10-bit pictures the video engine reconstructs into an 8-bit NV12
surface followed by a plane with the two low bits of every sample. Its
secondary (scale-down/rotate) output engine converts that reconstruction
into P010, which is what userspace gets: the P010 surface is placed at
the start of the capture buffer and the reconstruction behind it.

The reconstruction addressing, the buffer sizing and the secondary
output programming are the same for VP9 and H.265, which use the same
engine, so they are shared. P010 is offered for 10-bit streams only,
through the depth filter of the format list.

The hidden surface starts 1 KiB aligned and has a 64 byte aligned line
stride, which the VP9 decoder needs for its reconstruction addresses.
The plane of the two low bits has the same line stride in the
allocation and in the hardware: the width divided by 4, rounded up and
aligned to 32 bytes, as the vendor library programs it.

Signed-off-by: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
[aiyengar: rebased onto Armbian's "Implement AFBC YUV420 formats for H265":
 kept its AFBC chroma address check in cedrus_h265_frame_info_write_single()]
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
